WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:03.750 align:start position:0%
 
is worried  the farmer from poland north central sri  lanka says the yield from his paddy  field has dropped by nearly half  with debts to pay and costs rising every  day he says farmers are struggling  we have already pawned our tractors our  spraying machines our wives jewelry even  their wedding rings have been pawned  home rice cookers and sewing machines  have all been borrowed against  dharma tillaka and thousands like him  across the country blamed president  gotaber rajapaksa's decision last year  to ban chemical fertilizer pesticides  and we decides for the lower yields  officers from the statistics department  are assessing how much of the harvest  has been affected  one agriculture source told al jazeera  yields have fallen by 40 to 50 percent  because of the fertilizer ban the  president said the measure was aimed at  making sri lankans healthier and  switching to 100 organic agriculture  but it was removed six months later  after widespread protests  says the idea was a good one but the way  it was quickly implemented was wrong  farmers say they were not consulted  about the move and are angry at a  short-sighted experiment they say has  caused them dearly  we never thought this would happen in  poland which was so self-sufficient what  we want is to chase this government out  because of this injustice that i came  here today  the politicians who rode from 1977 must  take responsibility for this crisis they  are the ones who dragged our country  into the situation  the anger brought thousands of  protesters including dharmathelika to  polanaro in the north central province  the government especially the president  he clearly emphasized that this plan was  made  in order to establish organic farming  system in sri lanka but that is not the  reason so the government did not have  enough dollars to bring down fertilizers  from overseas so because of that this  problem was created and the farmers are  better they are better to the core most  farmers told al jazeera they rely on  their harvest to clear deaths and build  a stock of rice for their own use and  money to last them through till the next  harvest but let alone settling deaths  they now fear not having enough food for  their families  minister of agriculture mahinda nanda  aloot gamake has announced the  government will spend 200 million  dollars to help the farmers but the  farmers who are yet to receive the  payout say the rates do not cover their  losses  protests against the government's  inability to control rising prices and  address shortages of essential goods  have been increasing in recent months  the farming community has been growing  rice in these areas for hundreds of  years and say if the government cannot  address the current crisis it must  resign  mina fernandez al-jazeera colombo

